{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at the end.</b> Rashi explained: as the Targum renders — ״at the end,״ etc. — to say: if you interpret it according to Hebrew usage, the adversary has an opening to object and say it is approximate — ״and a portion of his brothers he took״ — and it would mean that when part of the two years had passed, the chief cupbearer was released. Now the Targum comes to teach us that when all two full years had passed, Pharaoh dreamed. And further, we find מקץ used in the sense of beginning: ״at the end of two years״ (מקץ שבע שנים) — but rather ״at the beginning of seven years you shall send each one his brother,״ in Jeremiah." "<b>Two years, days.</b> From the time the chief cupbearer went out from the prison house — meaning two full years. If it had written שנתים without ימים, I would say they are incomplete — at the end of one year and one day — like ״a year-old״ (בן שנתו), which during the year you call ״a year-old.״ Therefore it wrote ימים for you, to teach us to say: two years of full days, from day to day; and similarly ״a month of days״ (חדש ימים) — a full month of days. Another interpretation: <b>at the end of two years</b> — after the time decreed upon him to be in the prison house. As our Rabbis taught in tractate Sotah: he wanted to lie with Potiphar's wife, and the image of his father's likeness appeared to him, and he subdued his inclination and drove his fingernails into the ground, and seed came out of them. And Joseph was worthy to have twelve tribes issue from him, just as they issued from Jacob; but the seed came out from between his ten fingernails of his hands, so he begot only the two. Corresponding to them it was decreed upon him to be imprisoned ten years. And at the end of two years after this decree, <b>and Pharaoh dreamed,</b> etc. For we know that he was seventeen years old when he was sold, and one year he stayed in the Egyptian's house, as it is written ״in the house and in the field״ — ״in the house״ — these are the rainy days; ״in the field״ — these are the summer days. And when he stood before Pharaoh by means of the dream interpretation, he was thirty years old. If so, one must say that he was imprisoned twelve years."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {1 "In the Jeremiah citation, the commentator quotes מקץ שבע שנים from Jer. 34:14; the gloss 'at the beginning of seven years' reflects his argument that מקץ sometimes means the onset of the period, not its conclusion. The phrase ובעמדו לפני פרעה may also be rendered 'while standing before Pharaoh,' but the chronological argument favors 'when he stood.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:15.257610596Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}